      "title": "BoM forecasts strongest El Niño on record as research points to ‘alarming’ climate change influence",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/04/bom-forecasts-strongest-el-nino-on-record-as-research-points-to-alarming-climate-change-influence",
      "description": "<p>Bureau of Meteorology suggests El Niño has already broken records, with Pacific ocean temperature change exceeding 2C in six months</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/03/anthony-albanese-one-nation-jim-chalmers-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-tobacco-plan-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/mar/10/sign-up-for-the-clear-air-australia-environment-newsletter-with-adam-morton?CMP=cvau_sfl\">Sign up for climate and environment editor Adam Morton’s free Clear Air newsletter here</a></p></li></ul><p>The growing El Niño weather phenomenon to Australia’s east is already breaking heat records, with forecasts showing it will likely easily surpass previous events, according to Australia’s Bureau of Meteorology, as seperate research suggested climate change had greatly shortened the odds of the event hitting record extremes.</p><p>On Thursday <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/sep/03/el-nino-supersizing-before-our-eyes-united-nations\">the UN’s weather agency said the event in the Pacific was continuing to strengthen</a> and would probably persist until early 2027, warning it had the “potential to deliver a massive blow to communities and economies across the world”.</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/mar/10/sign-up-for-the-clear-air-australia-environment-newsletter-with-adam-morton?CMP=copyembed\">Sign up to get climate and environment editor Adam Morton’s Clear Air column as a free newsletter</a></strong></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/04/bom-forecasts-strongest-el-nino-on-record-as-research-points-to-alarming-climate-change-influence\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-03T15:00:51"
    },
    {
      "title": "Australian science prize awarded to researcher studying memory accuracy of domestic violence survivors",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/sep/03/eureka-prize-2026-helen-paterson-jane-tiller-australian-science-prizes",
      "description": "<p>Helen Paterson’s work on advancing how police and courts understand memory is recognised in prestigious Eureka prizes</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Memory is an imperfect thing: <a href=\"https://www.emerald.com/jcp/article/16/1/66/1251973/Facilitating-episodic-recall-for-repeated-events\">research shows</a> that recollections of single events – a purse being snatched, say – differ to those about repeated or ongoing events, such as incidents of domestic and family violence.</p><p>“What our research shows is that compared to a single-event witness, a repeated-event witness tends to report more gist-like details, the overall details of what typically would happen, and the things that remain constant between the episodes,” said Helen Paterson, an associate professor in forensic psychology at the University of Sydney.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/sep/03/eureka-prize-2026-helen-paterson-jane-tiller-australian-science-prizes\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-03T12:30:38"
    },
    {
      "title": "Property developer claimed ‘the premier is my best friend’ in tapped phone call, Icac hears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/03/icac-charles-perrottet-jean-nassif-nsw-liberals-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Charles Perrottet, the younger brother of the former premier, tells Icac that claims made by Jean Nassif in the call were ‘bravado’ and ‘far-fetched’</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/03/anthony-albanese-one-nation-jim-chalmers-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-tobacco-plan-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>A property developer who allegedly made prohibited donations to support factional Liberal work claimed “the premier is my best friend” in a secretly recorded phone call the day after Dominic Perrottet was chosen as New South Wales premier, an inquiry has heard.</p><p>Charles Perrottet, the younger brother of the former premier, told the Independent Commission Against Corruption (Icac) on Thursday that claims made by property developer Jean Nassif in a phone call lawfully intercepted by police were “bravado” and “far-fetched”.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/03/icac-charles-perrottet-jean-nassif-nsw-liberals-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-03T09:14:59"
    }
  ]
